{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfbhMD51QJK1oXjWqE89c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 03 is done: the pathwidth solver now has the repaired rules, and `repaired_rules=True` is the default in both solvers. The gate passes (lake build, 1 `sorry`, 1,343 MOSP tests, 110 pathwidth_solver tests).\n\n**What changed in `pathwidth_solver/pathwidth/`:**\n- **`closing_search.c`** (the 128-bit C) is again a byte copy of MOSP's `customer_search.c`, so it has item 02's change. The legacy wrapper calls `cs_decide_rules`.\n- **`closing_search_w.c`** (the multiword C, all word sizes from 2 to 16) has the same change. `csw_decide` gained a `repaired_rules` parameter.\n- **`search.py`** has the flag and, for the first time, a Python better move. Before this, `better_move` was silently ignored on the Python path. The code is the same as MOSP's.\n- **`native.py`** passes the flag through.\n- `src/customer_search.py`, the reference copy described as never edited, is untouched.\n\n**The default.** Both solvers' `decide` and `decide_native` default to `True`. Every caller that doesn't name the flag now runs the repaired rules, including `solve_mosp_exact`, `csearch`, `recertify` and `compute_pathwidth`. This is recorded at the top of `paper2/solver_fix.md`. The old C entry points kept for already-running processes still use the published rules.\n\nSeven MOSP tests failed after the flip. All of them pin counts or code written for the published rules. Four tools that model those rules (`learning/fix_cost.py`, `paper2/search_check.py`, `tests/test_fix_cost.py`, `tests/test_search_certificate.py`) now pass `repaired_rules=False`, and their pinned counts are unchanged. One of those failures shows the repair working: on the 10×13 instance, the `prefix` revert's false refutation at the optimum disappears under the repaired rules.\n\n**The check.** A new script, `paper2/solver_fix_pw_check.py`, sends each call through up to eight implementations: the pathwidth Python, the multiword C at each word size, the legacy C, and MOSP's Python and C. Status, node count and closing order must all match.\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Instances | 1,319 |\n| Sizes | 4–24 vertices at every `k`; corpus instances at 9–125 and sparse graphs at 129–992 under node caps |\n| Implementation-calls per setting | 6,291,160 |\n| Disagreements | **0** under either setting |\n| Old vs repaired giving a different answer | 0 (node counts differed on 4,151 pairs) |\n\nOver these capped runs the repaired rules took 0.02% more nodes. That is a hint, not a cost measurement; item 04 measures cost. New tests: `pathwidth_solver/tests/test_repaired_rules.py` (8 tests), and `test_identity_mosp.py` now runs under both settings.\n\n**No certified value changed and no false refutation turned up.** One thing for items 06–08: the certificate emitter in `learning/search_certificate.py` still models only the published rules, so anything compared against it needs the flag off. PROGRESS.md is updated (3/10) and the item is marked `[x]`. Nothing was committed and nothing was written to `solutions/`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":515,"cache_read_input_tokens":171713,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":515,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":754,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d026a0f9-f163-406f-b90d-147e6bda45b1","uuid":"e0136c4d-5858-4ca1-89c3-acae1f1064a2","timestamp":"2026-10-01T14:44:55.821Z","request_id":"req_011CfbhMCcxdYiDj1F4dQY43"}
